# Then CDC Made Things Even More Fascinating 🧠

While exploring FIFOs, another topic appears:

# CDC — Clock Domain Crossing

At first, the name sounds terrifying 😭

But the actual problem is surprisingly relatable.

Imagine two people trying to pass boxes to each other while walking at completely different speeds 📦

One person moves calmly.

The other moves like they had 7 coffees.

Without synchronization:

* timing breaks,
* boxes fall,
* coordination fails.

That is basically CDC inside chips.

Different hardware blocks operate on different clocks.

One may run at:

```text id="v2m7z5"
100 MHz
```

Another at:

```text id="x9p4n1"
250 MHz
```

Now data transfer becomes dangerous unless synchronization structures and asynchronous FIFOs are introduced.

And weirdly enough…

distributed systems start feeling conceptually similar again.

Different services.
Different execution timing.
Different processing speeds.

Both worlds are fundamentally trying to solve:

> How do independently moving systems communicate safely?

